-- examples/rofl-release/evidence.rofl — the observation journal, taken
-- 2026-09-11 on this checkout. Every entry names the command that produced it,
-- because an observation whose provenance is prose is a human_assertion
-- wearing a measurement's clothes.

-- `npm test` — 82 worlds, both engines, one committed golden, 12.7 s.
supports[obs](golden_82, two_engines_agree).
evidence_kind[obs](golden_82, measured).
evidence_version[obs](golden_82, "1.0.0").

-- THE BLOCKER MOVED AND DID NOT CLEAR, 2026-09-11. `why`, `whynot`, `retract`,
-- `excise`, `holds` and `fact_keys` are on `Session` and gated against the
-- reference host's own output, string for string, by
-- `rust/rofl/tests/explain.rs` (24 tests) -- so `d_port_owes_why` is
-- discharged and no longer reads UNCOVERED. What the claim still fails on is
-- narrower again with `evals`, and it is now TWO NAMES out of sixteen, both
-- with a written reason rather than a gap: `strataPlan`, which reads a
-- relation measured EMPTY in a settled store on both hosts, and
-- `assertClauses`, which `npm run features` reports reachable by no demo at
-- all. Recounted by walking both surfaces: 16 public on `Rofl`, 22 on
-- `Session`, and every other name answered.
refutes[obs](two_names_of_sixteen, port_surface_complete).
evidence_kind[obs](two_names_of_sixteen, measured).
evidence_version[obs](two_names_of_sixteen, "1.0.0").

-- AND THE OTHER SIDE, because a claim settled by deleting the evidence against
-- it is not settled. `rust/rofl/tests/explain.rs` is 24 tests holding the port
-- to the reference host's own output; the surface a caller actually reaches --
-- ask, assert, evaluate, tick, run, save, open, fork, why, whynot, retract,
-- excise, holds, fact_keys, and the residency verbs that are the port's alone
-- -- is answered. Both polarities stand and the disagreement is the answer:
-- whether two deliberately-refused names make a surface incomplete is the
-- decision authority's call, not the journal's.
supports[obs](surface_walk_22, port_surface_complete).
evidence_kind[obs](surface_walk_22, measured).
evidence_version[obs](surface_walk_22, "1.0.0").
supports[obs](explain_parity_16, port_explains).
evidence_kind[obs](explain_parity_16, measured).
evidence_version[obs](explain_parity_16, "1.0.0").

refutes[obs](speccheck_uncovered, every_duty_has_a_guard).
evidence_kind[obs](speccheck_uncovered, measured).
evidence_version[obs](speccheck_uncovered, "1.0.0").

-- `npm run features` — greedy cover reaches 52 of 53; `api:assertClauses` is
-- reachable by no demo.
refutes[obs](feature_census, every_feature_has_a_world).
evidence_kind[obs](feature_census, measured).
evidence_version[obs](feature_census, "1.0.0").

-- THE HOST KERNEL TAKES NOTHING, and the walk says so: no file under `src/` or
-- `runtime/` imports anything but `node:` builtins and itself. The Rust port
-- takes crates by construction, which is what a Rust library is and is not a
-- claim anybody made otherwise.
supports[obs](src_import_walk, js_kernel_takes_nothing).
evidence_kind[obs](src_import_walk, measured).
evidence_version[obs](src_import_walk, "1.0.0").

-- `@babel/parser` was in `dependencies` while its only importers are seven
-- files under `scanners/` and `scripts/`, neither of which ships -- so anybody
-- installing the engine pulled a parser it never calls. Moved to
-- devDependencies: the tarball now has NO dependencies, 20 files, 472 kB
-- unpacked.
supports[obs](package_manifest, package_declares_what_it_ships).
evidence_kind[obs](package_manifest, measured).
evidence_version[obs](package_manifest, "1.0.0").

-- LOAD. 5 676 864 facts over a 128-file world at 198 B/fact and 45.7 s
-- (docs/the-target.md, docs/port-surface.md), and the two walls a caller can
-- set and read -- `budget` in steps and `space` in rows -- are distinct atoms
-- demanding opposite repairs, with `examples/reach` built to show the
-- difference rather than assert it. The honest caveat travels with the number
-- and is in the doc: that is what THAT workload does, not what the engine does.
supports[obs](corpus_5_6m, holds_a_realistic_corpus).
evidence_kind[obs](corpus_5_6m, measured).
evidence_version[obs](corpus_5_6m, "1.0.0").

-- MONITORING, in the form a library can have it. A caller reads back
-- `partial`, `steps`, `peak_rows` against `space` -- how close to the wall,
-- reported WITHOUT a failure -- and per question `scanned`, `probed` and
-- `micros`, which is the difference between an index served and a relation
-- walked. A run that stops writes `hole(...)` naming WHY into the world
-- itself, so the record is a fact and not a log line.
supports[obs](caller_side_instruments, a_run_reports_on_itself).
evidence_kind[obs](caller_side_instruments, measured).
evidence_version[obs](caller_side_instruments, "1.0.0").

-- CUSTOMER ACCEPTANCE, AND IT IS THE ONE THAT FAILS. No user outside this
-- repository's author has built anything with it; the 21 demos, the 84 golden
-- worlds and the JS model are all the author's. Not inapplicable -- unmet.


-- START.md is `ROFL Kernel v0 — Implementation Handoff`, a pre-implementation
-- brief, and `npm run speccheck` reports 18 rows amending or qualifying it.
refutes[obs](start_md_amendments, release_contract_is_current).
evidence_kind[obs](start_md_amendments, measured).
evidence_version[obs](start_md_amendments, "1.0.0").

-- `docs_derived_from_ledger` HAS NO ENTRY ON PURPOSE. w_docs_from_ledger is
-- open in the worklist; absence of evidence does not derive falsehood here,
-- and the claim comes out `unknown` rather than refuted.

-- THE AUTHORITY'S OWN WORDS, as an observation rather than as a silent edit.
-- Kind `human_assertion` and not `measured`, because it is a judgement about
-- what a base version owes and not a count of anything. It does not settle the
-- claim -- the two measurements above still disagree and the claim still reads
-- CONTESTED -- and it was never meant to: what it settled is in frame.rofl.
supports[obs](owner_accepts_two_names, port_surface_complete).
evidence_kind[obs](owner_accepts_two_names, human_assertion).
evidence_version[obs](owner_accepts_two_names, "1.0.0").

-- ======================================================== THE OTHER DECISION
-- Measured 2026-09-11 by reading package.json and the tree, not by recalling
-- what either used to say.

-- THE ASKER'S ACCEPTANCE IS USE. 46 rule packs, 12 560 lines of ROFL, and the
-- instruments this repository is run with are models written in it: the
-- findings ledger, the worklist, the spec coverage, the feature census, the
-- layering and cleanliness audits, the dependency graph, the JS model of its
-- own source -- and this release decision. Whatever else is unproven, the
-- engine does real work daily for the person who asked for it.
supports[obs](self_application_46_packs, the_asker_runs_the_project_on_it).
evidence_kind[obs](self_application_46_packs, measured).
evidence_version[obs](self_application_46_packs, "1.0.0").

-- PROVEN BY DOING IT, 2026-09-11: `npm pack`, then `npm i` of the tarball into
-- an empty project, then `import { Rofl } from "rofl"` -- boot.rofl loaded,
-- a transitive closure evaluated, `why` printing its derivation tree, on plain
-- `node` with no flags. `main`, `exports`, `types` and `files` now exist and
-- the tarball is 20 files and no dependencies.
supports[obs](tarball_installed_and_used, installable_and_importable).
evidence_kind[obs](tarball_installed_and_used, measured).
evidence_version[obs](tarball_installed_and_used, "1.0.0").

-- AND THE `ship the TypeScript` FORK IS CLOSED BY MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN BY
-- TASTE. The first attempt shipped `src/*.ts` and Node refused it:
-- ERR_UNSUPPORTED_NODE_MODULES_TYPE_STRIPPING. Stripping is not applied under
-- `node_modules` in ANY version, so raw `.ts` works for this repository's own
-- scripts and cannot work for a dependency. `tsconfig.build.json` emits
-- `dist/` with declarations and maps; `src/` ships beside it to be read.
supports[obs](build_is_not_optional, package_declares_what_it_ships).
evidence_kind[obs](build_is_not_optional, measured).
evidence_version[obs](build_is_not_optional, "1.0.0").

-- The MIT text is in LICENSE and ships in the tarball, which is the one thing
-- the licence itself requires of a distribution.
supports[obs](licence_in_tarball, licence_text_ships).
evidence_kind[obs](licence_in_tarball, measured).
evidence_version[obs](licence_in_tarball, "1.0.0").

-- README now opens with `npm i rofl` and eleven lines that load boot, evaluate
-- a transitive closure and print a derivation tree -- the same program run
-- against the packed tarball in an empty project before it was written down,
-- so the snippet is a transcript rather than an intention. A line says where
-- the contributor half starts.
supports[obs](readme_opens_with_use, a_newcomer_can_run_a_world_from_the_readme).
evidence_kind[obs](readme_opens_with_use, measured).
evidence_version[obs](readme_opens_with_use, "1.0.0").

-- ONE NUMBER NOW, and it is the owner's call rather than a measurement: the
-- kernel showed what it is during the development of this tree, so 1.0.0 in
-- package.json, `ROFL v1` in the README, and START.md marked HISTORICAL --
-- which it always was, a pre-implementation handoff carried as a live
-- contract. What 1.0 PROMISES is the exported surface: `Rofl`, the port
-- client, and the language's closed vocabulary, which is mechanically checked.
supports[obs](one_version_number, the_version_means_something).
evidence_kind[obs](one_version_number, human_assertion).
evidence_version[obs](one_version_number, "1.0.0").

-- `bugs.url` points at the repository's issues, which is where a defect goes.
supports[obs](bugs_url, a_defect_has_somewhere_to_go).
evidence_kind[obs](bugs_url, measured).
evidence_version[obs](bugs_url, "1.0.0").

-- ADOPTION, recorded and gating nothing.
refutes[obs](no_outside_user_yet, used_by_somebody_other_than_its_author).
evidence_kind[obs](no_outside_user_yet, human_assertion).
evidence_version[obs](no_outside_user_yet, "1.0.0").
